---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import FileViewerDemo from "../../components/FileViewerDemo";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
---

<Docs
  title="File Viewer"
  description="Open local, linked, and Google files in a full-screen dialog or inline, with loading, error, empty, and unsupported states."
>
  <h1 class="docs-title">File Viewer</h1>
  <p class="docs-lead">
    Shows a file without leaving the page: images, PDF, text, JSON, Markdown,
    source code in any language, Google Docs links and shortcuts, plus
    spreadsheets and Office files through renderers you register. Use <code
      >FileViewer</code
    > for a full-screen dialog, <code>ViewableFileContent</code> inline, and
    <code>FileContent</code> when you load the bytes yourself.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-8"><FileViewerDemo client:load /></div>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Dialog</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Open an attachment"
      code={`import { FileAttachment } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/attachment";
import { FileViewer } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/file-viewer";

<FileAttachment file={attachment} preview viewable />

<FileViewer
  file={{ name: "Returns policy.pdf", mediaType: "application/pdf", source: { kind: "local", file } }}
  open={open}
  onOpenChange={setOpen}
/>`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>file.source</code> is <code>{'{ kind: "local", file }'}</code> for a
      <code>File</code> in browser memory or <code
        >{'{ kind: "remote", url }'}</code
      >
      for a link. The header shows the name and “Local file · Not uploaded”, “Linked
      file”, or “Google document”. Docs, Sheets, and Slides links on docs.google.com
      embed Google’s viewer and add Open in Google. Other files load up to 50 MB,
      time out after 15 seconds, and get Reload file and Download.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Stepping through files</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="One viewer for a set of files"
      code={`const [index, setIndex] = useState<number | null>(null);

<FileViewer
  files={files}
  index={index ?? 0}
  onIndexChange={setIndex}
  open={index !== null}
  onOpenChange={(open) => !open && setIndex(null)}
  finalFocus={() => buttons.current[index ?? 0]}
/>`}
    />
    <p>
      Pass <code>files</code>, <code>index</code>, and
      <code>onIndexChange</code> instead of <code>file</code>. With more than
      one file the header adds Previous file and Next file buttons and an “N of
      M” count. Left and Right arrows step too. The arrows stop at the first and
      last file. They are ignored while focus is in a text field, an embed, or a
      scrollable region, and when a modifier key is held.
      <code>finalFocus</code> picks where focus lands on close, usually the button
      for the file that was last on screen. <code>AttachmentGroup</code>
      wires this up for you.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Inline and your own bytes</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="A file page that fetches its own content"
      code={`import { FileContent, FileError, LoadingFile, renderableType } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/file-viewer";

const url = URL.createObjectURL(new Blob([bytes], { type: renderableType(name, mediaType) }));

<section aria-label="File content" className="flex h-160 flex-col overflow-hidden rounded-xl border">
  {content.kind === "loading" ? <LoadingFile />
    : content.kind === "failed" ? <FileError message="The file content couldn’t be loaded. Your file is unchanged." onRetry={retry} />
    : <FileContent name={name} mediaType={mediaType} bytes={content.bytes} url={url} onRetry={retry} />}
</section>`}
    />
    <p>
      All three fill a flex column, so give the parent a height.
      <code>renderableType</code> picks a safe type for the blob URL: PDF and raster
      images keep theirs, and anything a browser could run, including SVG, becomes
      <code>application/octet-stream</code>.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Images fit the frame; Actual size scrolls them at full resolution.
      </li>
      <li>
        Text, code, JSON, and Markdown source show in a <code>CodeBlock</code>
        with syntax colors and Copy. The language comes from the file name, such as
        <code>.py</code>, <code>.rs</code>, or <code>Dockerfile</code>. Code
        keeps line numbers; notes and Markdown wrap.
      </li>
      <li>
        JSON is pretty-printed. Invalid JSON shows its original text with a
        warning.
      </li>
      <li>
        Markdown is formatted, with a Show source toggle, when a Markdown
        renderer is registered.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>FilePreview</code> shows a small, non-interactive peek: the image, or
        the first 24 lines of text and code. <code>isPreviewableFile</code>
        says whether a file has one.
      </li>
      <li>
        Empty files, text that isn’t UTF-8, legacy <code>.ppt</code>, and other
        formats show a state that says what to do next.
      </li>
      <li>
        An embed that hasn’t loaded after 12 seconds offers Reload viewer.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Renderers</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Register spreadsheet, Office, and Markdown renderers once"
      code={`import { FileRenderersProvider } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/file-viewer";

const SpreadsheetFile = lazy(() => import("./spreadsheet-file"));
const OfficeFile = lazy(() => import("./office-file"));

<FileRenderersProvider
  renderers={{
    sheet: (props) => <SpreadsheetFile {...props} />,      // { name, bytes, onRetry }
    office: (props) => <OfficeFile {...props} />,          // { name, bytes, kind: "document" | "slides", onRetry }
    markdown: ({ text }) => <MessageResponse>{text}</MessageResponse>,
  }}
>
  <App />
</FileRenderersProvider>`}
    />
    <p>
      Spreadsheet and Office parsers are large, so Sprout doesn’t bundle them.
      Each renderer runs inside Suspense with <code>LoadingFile</code> as the fallback,
      so lazy components work as they are. Renderers can use
      <code>LoadingFile</code> and <code>FileError</code> for their own states. Any
      viewer also takes a <code>renderers</code> prop that overrides the provider.
      The demo above uses small sample renderers.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Accessibility</h2>
    <p>
      The dialog is titled by the file name and traps focus; Escape or “Close
      file viewer” closes it and returns focus to the button that opened it.
      Loading is announced as a status and errors as an alert, each with a Try
      again button. Actual size is a toggle button with
      <code>aria-pressed</code>, and the zoomed image is a scrollable, focusable
      region. Text and code scroll in a focusable region named after the
      language, such as “Python code”, and formatted Markdown in one named after
      the file. Show source is a toggle button with
      <code>aria-pressed</code>. When stepping, the new file’s name and
      position, such as “README.md, file 2 of 6”, are announced politely.
      Embedded frames are named after the file. Images fade in over 180ms and
      the slow-viewer notice over 120ms; with reduced motion both appear at
      once.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Theme tokens</h2>
    <p>
      The content area uses <code>--sprout-canvas</code>; pages, the header, and
      toolbars use <code>--sprout-surface</code> and
      <code>--sprout-line</code>. Warnings use <code>--sprout-danger</code>,
      links <code>--sprout-primary</code>, and focus rings
      <code>--sprout-focus</code>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Docs>
